{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Come, let us deal wisely with him</b> — Pharaoh and his wise counselors did not see fit to strike them with the sword, for it would be a great treachery to strike down for nothing the people who had come into the land by order of the first king. And also the people of the land would not permit the king to commit such violence, for he consults with them, and besides, the children of Israel are a numerous and mighty people, and they would wage a great war against them. But he said that they should act with wisdom so that the Israelites would not sense that they were acting with hostility against them, and therefore he imposed a tax upon them, for it is the way of strangers in the land to pay a tax to the king, as appears in Solomon's time (I Kings 9:21):<br><b>And afterward</b> he secretly commanded the midwives to kill the males on the birthstool, so that even the women giving birth themselves would not be aware of them, and afterward he commanded all his people: every son that is born, you shall cast into the Nile. And the point is that he did not wish to command his own executioners to kill them with the king's sword, or that they should cast them into the Nile, but rather he said to the people: whoever finds a Jewish child shall cast him into the Nile, and if the child's father cries out to the king or to the governor of the city, they will say to him: bring witnesses and exact retribution. And once the king's decree was issued, the Egyptians would search the houses and enter them at night and disguise themselves and take the children out of there, for therefore it is said: and she could no longer hide him (Exodus 2:3).<br><b>And it appears</b> that this lasted only a few days, for when Aaron was born the decree was not yet in force, and when Moses was born it appears to have been revoked — perhaps Pharaoh's daughter, in her compassion for him, told her father not to do such a thing, or when it became known that the matter originated from the king, he annulled it, or it was by decree of the astrologers, according to the words of our Sages (Exodus Rabbah 1:29). For all of this was their cunning against them, so that the violence would not be known. And this is the meaning of the statement of those who said to Moses our teacher: \"you have made our odor stink, to give a sword in their hands\" (Exodus 5:21) — for now they will increase their hatred toward us, and will find a pretext that we are rebelling against the kingdom, and they will kill us with the sword before everyone's eyes; they will no longer need to act with deception." "<b>and he will go up from the land</b> — against our will. And our Sages interpreted (Sotah 13a; Exodus Rabbah 1:9): like a person who curses himself and hangs his curse upon others — as if he had written \"and we shall go up from the land,\" and they will inherit it. This is the language of Rabbi Solomon [Rashi]. And if it were according to the Rav's [Ibn Ezra's] interpretation, it should have said \"and he will go up upon the land,\" for so is the language used for those who wage war: \"Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon went up\" (II Kings 24:1), \"against all the fortified cities of Judah and he captured them,\" \"and he seized them\" (II Kings 18:13), \"Rezin king of Aram went up to Jerusalem for war\" (Isaiah 7:1). And perhaps one may say: <b>and he will go up</b> against us <b>from the land</b> in which he dwells, hinting at the land of Goshen.<br><b>And it is possible</b> to interpret that he is saying: when wars are called, he will be added to our enemies to take spoil and to plunder booty, and he will go up from this land to the land of Canaan with all that is ours, and we will not be able to take our vengeance upon him and to fight him. And this is like the language of \"who brought us up from the land of Egypt\" (Exodus 32:1), \"who brought up and who brought the seed of the house of Israel from the land of the north and from all the lands\" (Jeremiah 23:8). And likewise: \"and they shall set themselves one head, and they shall go up from the land\" (Hosea 2:2 [English 2:1]) — that they shall set over themselves a head and go up to their land from the land where they were exiled."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The closing quotation in the first comment combines Pharaoh's words (Exodus 1:10) with the Israelite officers' complaint to Moses (Exodus 5:21); Ramban treats them together as part of one argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:30.764533365Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}